course: dictionary
lesson: 14816
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Qualify is one of them. The words around it tell you which meaning to pick, so use the context clues. «Our team qualified for the final.» Qualify means earned a place, because the team won its way into the final.
explain: «She qualified as a nurse after three years of training.» Here qualify means passed the training so she can do the job. The words as a nurse are the clue. Always ask: what does the sentence need? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
explain: Qualify can also mean counts as, as in «Does a tomato qualify as a fruit?» Another meaning is to make a statement less strong. In «Dogs usually bark at strangers», the word usually qualifies the claim, because not every dog does it every time.
fact: Qualify is a verb: qualify, qualifies, qualified, qualifying.
fact: «Qualify for» means to earn a place or a right, as in «qualify for the final».
fact: «Qualify as» means to pass the training or exams needed to do a job, as in «qualify as a nurse».
fact: «Qualify» can mean to count as something, as in «Does a tomato qualify as a fruit?»
fact: To qualify a statement means to add a word such as «usually» or «some» that makes it less strong.
exercise: «Maya qualified for the final by winning her semi.» What does qualified for mean here? | *Earned a place in the final | Was given a new kit | Lost her place in the final | Watched the final from the stands
exercise: «Ben qualified as a plumber after two years of training.» What does qualified as mean here? | Wrote a book about pipes | Stopped his training | Bought some tools | *Passed the training to do the job
exercise: «Does a potato qualify as a vegetable?» What does qualify mean here? | Pay for it | Cook it | *Count as | Argue loudly
test: «Children qualify for free lunch if their family earns less than a set amount.» What does qualify for mean? | Pay double | *Meet the rules and be allowed to get it | Get a place in the lunch queue | Lose the right to lunch
test: «Most birds can fly.» What does the word most do to this statement? | Adds a fact about feathers | Makes it stronger and certain | Turns it into a question | *Makes it less strong, so it is not always true
exam: «A number qualifies for the list if it is a multiple of 5.» What does qualifies mean? | Is written in capitals | Is very large | *Meets the condition, so it counts | Is cut off
